    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d357">

                <pb facs="0557" n="271" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg271"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 341.622a/145</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Gerard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-06-03T16:00:00+02:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 3, 1916, 4 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received June 4, 4.05 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3951. In conversation with Von Jagow
                    yesterday he referred to fact that copies of American notes to England such as
                        <hi rendition="#italic">China</hi> steamer case, taking of mails,
                        etc.,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d357-f1"/> were not published, while American
                    notes to Germany were invariably published, often before received. He said that
                    he thought it would do much good if these notes were published here.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gerard</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d357-f1" n="2" xml:id="d357-f1-content">
                <p>See the sections dealing with “The Removal by Belligerent Naval Authorties of
                    Enemy Subjects and Military Reservists from American Ships on the High Seas,”
                        <hi rendition="#italic">post</hi>, <ref target="#pg630">p. 630</ref>, and
                    “Interference with the Mails by Belligerent Governments,” <hi
                        rendition="#italic">post</hi>, <ref target="#pg591">p. 591</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
